30
00:02:17,086 --> 00:02:18,498
After this video you can:

31
00:02:18,838 --> 00:02:23,431
Establish what a hard reset destroyed and
what the reflog and dangling blobs still

32
00:02:23,471 --> 00:02:23,713
hold.

33
00:02:24,053 --> 00:02:27,734
Recover the committed and the staged
work and state honestly what is gone.

34
00:02:28,074 --> 00:02:32,744
Classify a "disappeared" branch: deleted
locally, pruned remote-tracking ref, never

35
00:02:32,784 --> 00:02:33,652
pushed, renamed.

36
00:02:33,992 --> 00:02:36,284
Find the tip, anchor it
and restore the branch.

37
00:02:36,624 --> 00:02:38,848
Write the summary and
the prevention for both.

38
00:02:40,688 --> 00:02:44,196
Incident 1: what a hard reset
does to three kinds of work.

39
00:02:44,236 --> 00:02:49,090
git reset --hard moves the branch, replaces
the index and overwrites the working

40
00:02:49,130 --> 00:02:54,106
tree. The index is the proposed next commit,
and the working tree is the files you

41
00:02:54,146 --> 00:02:58,939
edit. The work that was there before sits
on three layers, and each layer has a

42
00:02:58,979 --> 00:02:59,979
different answer.

43
00:03:00,319 --> 00:03:05,381
Committed work is in the reflog. Staged
work was written to the object database by

44
00:03:05,421 --> 00:03:10,607
git add, so it survives as a blob, Git's
object for one file's content, that nothing

45
00:03:10,647 --> 00:03:15,771
references. A blob has no file name: the
name was in the index entry that the reset

46
00:03:15,811 --> 00:03:20,811
removed. Unstaged work was never given to
Git, and Git can't return what it never

47
00:03:20,851 --> 00:03:21,247
stored.

48
00:03:21,587 --> 00:03:26,716
The root cause, as the textbook states it.
Ravi wanted to discard one edit and used

49
00:03:26,756 --> 00:03:31,947
a command that moves the branch, replaces
the index and overwrites the working tree.

50
00:03:31,987 --> 00:03:36,493
origin/main, his clone's record of the
server's main, wasn't his branch's

51
00:03:36,533 --> 00:03:40,229
counterpart. No counterpart
existed. Layer: Git, one laptop.

52
00:03:40,269 --> 00:03:41,786
git pull wasn't involved.

53
00:03:42,126 --> 00:03:46,574
Why the tempting recovery is wrong. The
tempting move is to put the branch back

54
00:03:46,614 --> 00:03:49,869
where it was before the accident,
with another hard reset.

55
00:03:49,909 --> 00:03:54,470
But Ravi has committed once since the
reset. Moving the branch back would discard

56
00:03:54,510 --> 00:03:55,152
that commit.

57
00:03:55,492 --> 00:04:01,448
So the three commits are added on top instead.
This is the ladder of video 184: add,

58
00:04:01,488 --> 00:04:02,233
don't move.

59
00:04:02,573 --> 00:04:06,045
Incident 8: "disappeared" is a
symptom with several mechanisms.

60
00:04:06,085 --> 00:04:10,449
A branch can be missing because it was
renamed. Because it was deleted locally.

61
00:04:10,489 --> 00:04:14,406
Because the server's branch was deleted
and a pruning fetch removed the

62
00:04:14,446 --> 00:04:17,472
remote-tracking ref, the
clone's record of that branch.

63
00:04:17,512 --> 00:04:22,155
Or because it was never pushed. Usually more
than one of these has happened, and the

64
00:04:22,195 --> 00:04:26,391
question that matters isn't "where is
the branch" but "which work is where".

65
00:04:26,731 --> 00:04:30,200
The root cause here: four
steps, each reasonable alone.

66
00:04:30,240 --> 00:04:35,559
GitHub squash-merged the pull request, the
proposal to merge her branch, and deleted

67
00:04:35,599 --> 00:04:40,663
the head branch. A squash merge combines
a branch's commits into one new commit.

68
00:04:40,703 --> 00:04:45,575
fetch.prune removed the remote-tracking
ref. Work had continued on the merged

69
00:04:45,615 --> 00:04:50,870
branch. git branch -d refused, correctly,
because squashed commits aren't ancestors

70
00:04:50,910 --> 00:04:56,229
of main, and -D overrode the refusal. Layers:
GitHub for the first step, Git for the

71
00:04:56,269 --> 00:04:56,548
rest.

72
00:04:56,888 --> 00:05:01,782
Two tools, one of which misleads. git cherry
compares patch IDs, which are hashes of

73
00:05:01,822 --> 00:05:06,364
each commit's change, and a squashed
commit equals none of its parts, so every

74
00:05:06,404 --> 00:05:11,298
original commit looks unmerged. Trees, the
complete snapshots, are reliable: compare

75
00:05:11,338 --> 00:05:14,352
the content of the old tip
with the content of main.

76
00:05:14,692 --> 00:05:19,469
GitHub, not Git. A closed pull request
offers "Restore branch" for its head branch.

77
00:05:19,509 --> 00:05:24,344
It restores what the server had. A commit
that was never pushed isn't on the server,

78
00:05:24,384 --> 00:05:26,027
and Support can't produce it.

79
00:05:26,367 --> 00:05:31,696
Why the tempting recovery is wrong here
too. Recreating and pushing the old branch

80
00:05:31,736 --> 00:05:36,868
would publish three commits whose content
is already in main: the reused-branch

81
00:05:36,908 --> 00:05:38,243
problem of video 125.

82
00:05:38,583 --> 00:05:42,690
The safe recovery is a new branch from
the current main with the one commit that

83
00:05:42,730 --> 00:05:43,364
isn't merged.

84
00:05:45,204 --> 00:05:49,906
Think of the three layers of Incident 1
as three kinds of paper on a desk that

85
00:05:49,946 --> 00:05:54,830
somebody swept clean. Pages that were
filed, the commits, are in the archive, and

86
00:05:54,870 --> 00:05:57,931
the archive has an index
card for each: the reflog.

87
00:05:57,971 --> 00:06:02,977
Pages that were put in the out-tray, the
staged file, were photocopied by the clerk

88
00:06:03,017 --> 00:06:06,625
before the sweep. The copy
exists, without its folder label.

89
00:06:06,665 --> 00:06:10,272
Pages you were still writing on
were never copied by anyone.

90
00:06:10,612 --> 00:06:14,448
Where the picture breaks: the
archive's index cards expire.

91
00:06:14,488 --> 00:06:19,966
By default a reflog entry for a commit that's
no longer reachable lasts 30 days, and

92
00:06:20,006 --> 00:06:25,418
a dangling blob goes two weeks after it
becomes unreachable, once maintenance runs.

93
00:06:25,458 --> 00:06:29,228
The lab switches reflog expiry
off. A real laptop doesn't.

94
00:06:29,568 --> 00:06:35,282
Try it now. Thirty seconds, read-only. In
the lab shell or any repository you have,

95
00:06:35,322 --> 00:06:39,234
run git reflog -5, and read
the lines from the bottom up.

96
00:06:41,574 --> 00:06:46,205
Each line is one move of HEAD, Git's note
of where you are: the commit it arrived

97
00:06:46,245 --> 00:06:49,549
at, and the command behind it.
Those are your index cards.

98
00:06:49,889 --> 00:06:55,235
For Incident 8, hold the sentence from
video 170: "merged" in Git means "reachable

99
00:06:55,275 --> 00:06:59,176
from". A squash keeps the content
and discards the ancestry.

100
00:06:59,216 --> 00:07:04,628
So every tool that asks about ancestry or
patch identity says "not merged", and the

101
00:07:04,668 --> 00:07:07,781
tool that compares content
says "already there".

102
00:07:09,621 --> 00:07:14,296
Before the demo, here is everything we
know so far from the people involved: two

103
00:07:14,336 --> 00:07:16,123
reports, each naming a suspect.

104
00:07:16,463 --> 00:07:19,835
Each incident gets a timeline,
built from a reflog.

105
00:07:19,875 --> 00:07:23,648
We draw both in the demo, once
the reflogs are on screen.

106
00:07:25,488 --> 00:07:29,133
Last call. From here on the
screen shows the solutions.

107
00:07:29,173 --> 00:07:31,478
Is your own command log beside you?

108
00:07:33,818 --> 00:07:35,130
Then compare as we go.

109
00:07:35,470 --> 00:07:37,053
Observe. 🟢 SAFE.

110
00:07:37,393 --> 00:07:42,122
git status -sb prints the branch name with
no upstream, that is, no server branch

111
00:07:42,162 --> 00:07:47,008
that it follows. So there's no "this branch
on the server", and "I reset to what is

112
00:07:47,048 --> 00:07:51,954
on the server" can't mean what Ravi thinks.
The graph shows four commits and none of

113
00:07:51,994 --> 00:07:52,484
Friday's.

114
00:07:52,824 --> 00:07:57,565
The reflog. Predict: will the
HEAD reflog contain a pull line?

115
00:07:57,905 --> 00:08:03,487
Three hypotheses, separated by this one
output. A pull rewrote the branch: no pull

116
00:08:03,527 --> 00:08:08,903
line exists, the count is 0. The commits
were made elsewhere: no, three commit:

117
00:08:08,943 --> 00:08:14,388
lines are in this branch's reflog. A reset
moved the branch away from them: @{1}

118
00:08:14,428 --> 00:08:19,942
says "reset: moving to origin/main". And
@{0} is the commit Ravi made afterwards.

119
00:08:20,282 --> 00:08:25,571
Here's that reflog as a timeline, oldest
at the top: the last good tip at @{2}, the

120
00:08:25,611 --> 00:08:30,644
destructive moment at @{1}. Also lost
there: a staged file, which survives as a

121
00:08:30,684 --> 00:08:34,497
dangling blob, and an unstaged
edit, which survives nowhere.

122
00:08:34,837 --> 00:08:37,874
Anchor. 🟢 SAFE: a branch
at the last good tip.

123
00:08:38,214 --> 00:08:41,417
The three commits have a name
now, whatever happens next.

124
00:08:41,757 --> 00:08:48,478
The staged file. git fsck --lost-found is
the diagnostic command that isn't strictly

125
00:08:48,518 --> 00:08:52,503
read-only: it writes files
under .git/lost-found/.

126
00:08:52,843 --> 00:08:57,895
One dangling blob, 959c205, and
its content is the priority rules.

127
00:08:57,935 --> 00:09:03,835
It has no file name. Ravi supplies the
name from memory: rules/priority.yaml.

128
00:09:04,175 --> 00:09:08,846
Recover. 🟡 CAUTION: git cherry-pick adds
commits to the current branch: it copies

129
00:09:08,886 --> 00:09:13,674
each commit's change as a new commit.
Predict: why a cherry-pick of a range and not

130
00:09:13,714 --> 00:09:15,360
a reset to the rescue branch?

131
00:09:15,700 --> 00:09:19,887
The three commits are added on top of
the commit made after the reset, and the

132
00:09:19,927 --> 00:09:22,217
blob's content is written
back to its path.

133
00:09:22,557 --> 00:09:23,208
Verify.

134
00:09:23,548 --> 00:09:28,534
git range-diff shows three pairs marked
with an equals sign: each copy carries the

135
00:09:28,574 --> 00:09:33,377
same change as its original. The first
line, with the arrow, is the commit made

136
00:09:33,417 --> 00:09:37,116
after the reset, which the
tempting recovery would have lost.

137
00:09:37,156 --> 00:09:42,080
Then the check script: every line ok, and
PASS. Compare with the failing check of

138
00:09:42,120 --> 00:09:46,923
video 185: the lines that failed there
are the commit made after the reset, the

139
00:09:46,963 --> 00:09:49,252
relation to main, and the staged file.

140
00:09:52,592 --> 00:09:57,225
🔴 DANGEROUS: git branch -D. It deletes
the branch reflog and the only name of

141
00:09:57,265 --> 00:10:02,020
commits that aren't merged elsewhere. The
recovery is the HEAD reflog, until it

142
00:10:02,060 --> 00:10:07,118
expires. It's appropriate here because the
range-diff has shown that every commit on

143
00:10:07,158 --> 00:10:12,216
the rescue branch has its copy on the real
branch. The rescue branch is deleted only

144
00:10:12,256 --> 00:10:16,161
after the verification, and the
deletion prints the ID once more.

145
00:10:16,501 --> 00:10:21,885
The message to Ravi. What is back: three
commits, and one file as untracked content.

146
00:10:21,925 --> 00:10:26,727
What isn't: the unstaged edit to the
routing rules, which Git never stored.

147
00:10:26,767 --> 00:10:30,859
And: git pull wasn't involved.
That's the first suspect cleared.

148
00:10:30,899 --> 00:10:32,667
Nobody else needs a message.

149
00:10:33,007 --> 00:10:33,582
Observe.

150
00:10:33,922 --> 00:10:38,933
No ref of that name exists anywhere: not
locally, not as a remote-tracking branch,

151
00:10:38,973 --> 00:10:40,042
not on the server.

152
00:10:40,382 --> 00:10:45,167
The reflogs. Quick quiz: which
of the two reflogs still exists?

153
00:10:45,207 --> 00:10:49,073
A, the reflog of the branch.
B, the reflog of HEAD.

154
00:10:49,113 --> 00:10:49,992
Your answer?

155
00:10:54,832 --> 00:11:00,113
B. The branch reflog went with the branch.
The HEAD reflog is still here, and it

156
00:11:00,153 --> 00:11:03,971
holds the four commits and the
moment she left the branch.

157
00:11:04,011 --> 00:11:06,498
The last commit made on it is 70df7f7.

158
00:11:06,838 --> 00:11:07,741
Why is it gone?

159
00:11:08,081 --> 00:11:12,546
The newest commit on main has Asha as
author and Ravi as committer, and a pull

160
00:11:12,586 --> 00:11:17,340
request number in its subject: the shape
of a squash merge, which keeps none of the

161
00:11:17,380 --> 00:11:22,019
original messages. That's why none of her
commit messages are in the log of main.

162
00:11:22,359 --> 00:11:24,150
Classify: which work is where?

163
00:11:24,490 --> 00:11:28,113
Predict what git cherry will say,
and whether you should believe it.

164
00:11:28,153 --> 00:11:28,974
Say it out loud.

165
00:11:33,814 --> 00:11:38,592
git cherry reports all four commits as
unmerged, which is misleading: a squashed

166
00:11:38,632 --> 00:11:41,723
commit equals none of its
parts. Trees are reliable.

167
00:11:41,763 --> 00:11:46,601
The old tip differs from main by one file.
The commit before it doesn't differ at

168
00:11:46,641 --> 00:11:51,599
all. So three commits are in main as one,
and the fourth, the validation, was never

169
00:11:51,639 --> 00:11:56,416
pushed. "I am sure everything was pushed"
was true of three commits out of four.

170
00:11:56,756 --> 00:12:02,031
And here's the timeline of Incident 8, from
the reflog of HEAD, because the branch's

171
00:12:02,071 --> 00:12:04,056
own reflog went with the branch.

172
00:12:04,396 --> 00:12:07,852
Anchor, then recover. 🟢 SAFE for
the anchor and the new branch.

173
00:12:07,892 --> 00:12:11,127
🟡 CAUTION for the cherry-pick
and the push of a new branch.

174
00:12:15,467 --> 00:12:20,091
A new branch from the current main, with
the one commit that isn't merged, under a

175
00:12:20,131 --> 00:12:20,603
new name.

176
00:12:20,943 --> 00:12:21,594
Verify.

177
00:12:24,934 --> 00:12:28,880
A pull request for the new branch
would list one commit and one file.

178
00:12:28,920 --> 00:12:33,732
The diff between the rescue branch and the
new branch prints nothing: the new branch

179
00:12:33,772 --> 00:12:38,527
has everything the lost one had. Then the
rescue branch goes, and the check passes.

180
00:12:38,567 --> 00:12:42,051
Read its last line: "the merged
branch was not pushed again".

181
00:12:42,391 --> 00:12:45,709
The message to Asha. Nothing
was deleted by mistake.

182
00:12:45,749 --> 00:12:50,424
Three commits are in main as one. The
fourth survived only in her reflog.

183
00:12:50,464 --> 00:12:53,654
So the server, the second
suspect, is cleared too.

184
00:12:55,494 --> 00:12:57,142
Five mistakes to watch for.

185
00:12:57,482 --> 00:13:02,316
Accepting the suspect named in the report.
Root cause: "pull ate my work" and "the

186
00:13:02,356 --> 00:13:07,131
server lost my branch" are interpretations;
the reflog had no pull entry, and the

187
00:13:07,171 --> 00:13:09,212
server never had the fourth commit.

188
00:13:09,552 --> 00:13:12,610
Recovering from a hard reset
with another hard reset.

189
00:13:12,650 --> 00:13:17,404
Root cause: the branch has moved on since
the accident, so moving it back discards

190
00:13:17,444 --> 00:13:18,983
the commit made afterwards.

191
00:13:19,323 --> 00:13:24,347
Promising to recover everything. Root cause:
unstaged work was never written to the

192
00:13:24,387 --> 00:13:27,763
object database, so there is
nothing to recover it from.

193
00:13:28,103 --> 00:13:33,051
Trusting git cherry after a squash merge.
Root cause: patch IDs compare individual

194
00:13:33,091 --> 00:13:36,457
commits, and a squashed commit
equals none of its parts.

195
00:13:36,797 --> 00:13:41,520
Recreating and pushing the merged branch.
Root cause: its commits are not ancestors

196
00:13:41,560 --> 00:13:45,652
of main although their content is
there, so a pull request from it shows

197
00:13:45,692 --> 00:13:47,144
already-merged work again.

198
00:13:48,984 --> 00:13:53,556
Now, out of the lab. A prompt-engineering
team uses squash merges and automatic

199
00:13:53,596 --> 00:13:58,110
deletion of head branches. An engineer
comes back from two days off, finds her

200
00:13:58,150 --> 00:14:02,021
branch gone everywhere, and can't
find her commit messages on main.

201
00:14:02,061 --> 00:14:06,225
She is certain that the server lost
her work, and she is about to ask an

202
00:14:06,265 --> 00:14:10,020
administrator to restore the branch
from the closed pull request.

203
00:14:10,360 --> 00:14:15,129
A colleague asks one question first: "Did
you commit anything after your last push?"

204
00:14:15,169 --> 00:14:19,825
Then he reads the reflog of HEAD with her.
The answer is on the screen: one commit

205
00:14:19,865 --> 00:14:24,634
after the last push. Restoring the server's
branch would have brought back the three

206
00:14:24,674 --> 00:14:29,444
commits that are already in main and not
the one she needs, because the server never

207
00:14:29,484 --> 00:14:29,845
had it.

208
00:14:30,185 --> 00:14:35,070
They anchor the tip, compare trees, and
move the one unmerged commit to a new

209
00:14:35,110 --> 00:14:39,612
branch. The team's notes get two
lines. A new branch after every merge.

210
00:14:39,652 --> 00:14:43,322
And "pushed" is read from git
status -sb, not from memory.

211
00:14:43,362 --> 00:14:48,439
The colleague adds the line from the
